 our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 12 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 12 & -10 & 8\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 12 & -10 & 6 \\\\ 12 & -10 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 12{x}_{1}-10{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 12{x}_{1}-10{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -10 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -10 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -15 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=3{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{4}(-5{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(-5(3{x}_{3})+7{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361786