s an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361792 1113 #uuid "c9a5bc14-9aaf-5e50-ac9e-ba8b81836ace" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361792 1145 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}21 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -12 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -7\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}20{x}_{1}-13{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}-13{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 18{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{9}{10}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{20}(13{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}(13(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3})+9{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361793 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361794 13194139535696 true] [17592186361793 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361795 13194139535696 true] [17592186361793 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361796 13194139535696 true] [17592186361793 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361797 13194139535696 true] [17592186361793 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361798 13194139535696 true] [17592186361793 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535696 true] [17592186361793 1041 "selection" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361793 1104 #edb/ref 17592186361794 13194139535696 true] [17592186361793 1110 #uuid "3153942a-88df-560b-93c6-1ff857a89306" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361794 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361794 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535696 true] [17592186361794 1111 #uuid "2655f0b7-0ac1-5fb0-b4bb-7f69895b00f4" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361795 1034 "3" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361795 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535696 true] [17592186361795 1111 #uuid "d4212a08-7dc9-504f-a39d-65b9efe1f20d" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361796 1034 "2" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361796 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535696 true] [17592186361796 1111 #uuid "f3bca541-ffa2-546d-bb43-ab8450bb9955" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361797 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361797 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535696 true] [17592186361797 1111 #uuid "0aa1afd8-15e3-57b6-8a65-e6f04fc0a721" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361798 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361798 1103 #