aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-6.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-6.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-6.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-6)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=-{x}_{3}.$ From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{3}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.$" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361828 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361829 13194139535696 true] [17592186361828 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361830 13194139535696 true] [17592186361828 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361831 13194139535696 true] [17592186361828 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361832 13194139535696 true] [17592186361828 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361833 13194139535696 true] [17592186361828 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535696 true] [17592186361828 1041 "selection" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361828 1104 #edb/ref 17592186361833 13194139535696 true] [17592186361828 1110 #uuid "11eee310-ed78-5fbe-93ef-41616b28ac33" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361829 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361829 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535696 true] [17592186361829 1111 #uuid "fa61dc56-1cac-5008-b469-8fe5b67f69d8" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361830 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361830 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535696 true] [17592186361830 1111 #uuid "21c5b8bf-6781-57ae-b25d-3beddbf4a42c" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361831 1034 "2" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361831 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535696 true] [17592186361831 1111 #uuid "759db6d3-bc1e-5ec8-b61a-4461d47529a8" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361832 1034 "-1" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361832 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535696 true] [17592186361832 1111 #uuid "b82625de-591e-562c-aa0d-f0662fac097b" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361833 1034 "-2" 13194139535696 true] [17592186361833 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535696 true] [17592186361833 1111 #uuid "72da